ing; God, Christ; husband”), frēo (“woman”), from Proto-Indo-European *proHwo-, a derivation from *per- (“to go forward”).\nCognate with Old Saxon frūa (hence Middle Low German vrouwe, Modern Low German frug), Old Norse freyja and frú (hence Icelandic frú and Norwegian frue). Further cognates include German Fron (“corvée, soccage”) and its kin. The Indo-European root is also the source of Proto-Slavic *pravъ (“right, straight, correct”), whence Old Church Slavonic правъ (pravŭ), Russian пра́вый (právyj, “right”), perhaps also Ancient Greek πρῶρα (prôra, “bow, prow”) and the first element of Latin provincia, if this is from Proto-Indo-European *prōwiōn.",{"g":"f","decl":"||/en|/en|en|en|en|en"}]],"senses":[[4587,"frau","noun","woman (adult female human)",0,"f"],[4588,"frau","noun","wife (married woman, especially in relation to her spouse)",1,"f"],[4589,"frau","noun","a title of courtesy, equivalent to Mrs/Ms, which has nearly replaced Fräulein in the function of Miss",2,"f"],[4590,"frau","noun","madam (polite form of address for a woman or lady)",3,"f"],[4591,"frau","noun","lady; noblewoman (woman of breeding or higher class)",4,"f"],[4592,"frau","pron","one, they (indefinite third-person singular pronoun)",5,null,["informal","slang","rare","humorous"]]],"related_forms":[[7419,"frau","alibifrau","derived"],[7420,"frau","buhfrau","derived"],[7421,"frau","ehefrau","derived"],[7422,"frau","ersatzfrau","derived"],[7423,"frau","feuerwehrfrau","derived"],[7424,"frau","frauchen","derived"],[7425,"frau","frauenabend","derived"],[7426,"frau","frauenarbeit","derived"],[7427,"frau","frauenarzt","derived"],[7428,"frau","frauenarzthelferin","derived"],[7429,"frau","frauenbeauftragte","derived"],[7430,"frau","frauenbeilage","derived"],[7431,"frau","frauenbelange","derived"],[7432,"frau","frauenberuf","derived"],[7433,"frau","frauenbevorzugung","derived"],[7434,"frau","frauenbewegung","derived"],[7435,"frau","frauenbuchladen","derived"],[7436,"frau","frauenchor","derived"],[7437,"